  "answers": [
    {
      "owner": {
        "account_id": 2821493,
        "reputation": 23453,
        "user_id": 2425163,
        "user_type": "registered",
        "accept_rate": 75,
        "profile_image": "https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/05f4000597865eeaf51c32ed5694479c?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG",
        "display_name": "tarleb",
        "link": "https://stackoverflow.com/users/2425163/tarleb"
      },
      "is_accepted": true,
      "score": 1,
      "last_activity_date": 1712057255,
      "creation_date": 1712057255,
      "answer_id": 78260959,
      "question_id": 78258237,
      "content_license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "body": "<p>Of all the table formats supported by pandoc Markdown, there's only one that supports tables with footer rows: <a href=\"https://pandoc.org/MANUAL#extension-grid_tables\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">grid tables</a>. The below should give the desired results (requires pandoc 3 or newer).</p>\n<pre><code>+-----+-----+-----+-----+\n| ABC | DEF | GHI | JKL |\n+:===:+:===:+:===:+:===:+\n| 1   | 123 | 456 | 789 |\n+-----+-----+-----+-----+\n| 2   | 234 | 567 | 890 |\n+-----+-----+-----+-----+\n| 3   | 345 | 678 | 901 |\n+=====+=====+=====+=====+\n|     | 111 | 222 | 333 |\n+=====+=====+=====+=====+\n  : Foo bar baz quux, mumble blurf.\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/H8H5A.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/H8H5A.png\" alt=\"Table with footer\" /></a></p>\n<hr />\n<p>Also note that, if you have more specific requirements, you could always try to use a LaTeX table instead of a Markdown table, and then use the <a href=\"https://github.com/tarleb/parse-latex\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">parse-latex</a> Lua filter to parse it when converting to other formats.</p>\n"
    },
    {
      "owner": {
        "account_id": 2821493,
        "reputation": 23453,
        "user_id": 2425163,
        "user_type": "registered",
        "accept_rate": 75,
        "profile_image": "https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/05f4000597865eeaf51c32ed5694479c?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG",
        "display_name": "tarleb",
        "link": "https://stackoverflow.com/users/2425163/tarleb"
      },
      "is_accepted": false,
      "score": 1,
      "last_activity_date": 1712058277,
      "creation_date": 1712058277,
      "answer_id": 78261053,
      "question_id": 78258237,
      "content_license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "body": "<p>A hacky solution, but one that should also work with the oldish pandoc 2.9, is to insert the horizontal line with a raw LaTeX command in the table. It works, but the results aren't as pleasant, because the line isn't vertically centered between the rows.</p>\n<pre><code>|  ABC  |  DEF  |  GHI  |  JKL  |\n|:-----:|:-----:|:-----:|:-----:|\n|  1    |  123  | 456   |  789  |\n|  2    |  234  | 567   |  890  |\n|  3    |  345  | 678   |  901  |\n|`\\hline`{=tex} |  111  | 222   |  333  |\n  : Foo bar baz quux, mumble blurf.\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/22zUI.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/22zUI.png\" alt=\"view of the table, using the \\hline hack.\" /></a></p>\n"
    },
    {
      "owner": {
        "account_id": 39208,
        "reputation": 270833,
        "user_id": 112968,
        "user_type": "registered",
        "accept_rate": 68,
        "profile_image": "https://i.sstatic.net/zHTaT.png?s=256",
        "display_name": "knittl",
        "link": "https://stackoverflow.com/users/112968/knittl"
      },
      "is_accepted": true,
      "score": 1,
      "last_activity_date": 1712129957,
      "creation_date": 1712129957,
      "answer_id": 78266023,
      "question_id": 78262117,
      "content_license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "body": "<blockquote>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/jqlang/jq\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">jq</a> is a lightweight and flexible command-line <em>JSON</em> processor akin to sed,awk,grep, and friends for <em>JSON</em> data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>While YAML is a superset of JSON (any JSON document is also a valid YAML document), the reverse is not true. This means you cannot process generic YAML files with jq, but you need a tool that can handle YAML. One such tool is <a href=\"https://github.com/mikefarah/yq\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">yq</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>a lightweight and portable command-line YAML, JSON and XML processor. yq uses jq like syntax but works with yaml files as well as json, xml, properties, csv and tsv.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Since it uses a jq like syntax, you can simply swap the command:</p>\n<pre><code>helm show chart … -n test | yq .\n</code></pre>\n"
    },
    {
      "owner": {
        "account_id": 39208,
        "reputation": 270833,
        "user_id": 112968,
        "user_type": "registered",
        "accept_rate": 68,
        "profile_image": "https://i.sstatic.net/zHTaT.png?s=256",
        "display_name": "knittl",
        "link": "https://stackoverflow.com/users/112968/knittl"
      },
      "is_accepted": false,
      "score": 0,
      "last_activity_date": 1712130404,
      "creation_date": 1712130404,
      "answer_id": 78266070,
      "question_id": 78253195,
      "content_license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "body": "<p>To convert all values of an array, you need to <a href=\"https://jqlang.github.io/jq/manual/#map-map_values\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>map</code></a> its elements:</p>\n<pre><code>.DailyForecasts | map(.Day.RainProbability)\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>map(f)</code> is equivalent to <code>[.[]|f]</code>, so the above can be rewritten to the equivalent:</p>\n<pre><code>[.DailyForecasts.[].Day.RainProbability]\n</code></pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-json prettyprint-override\"><code>[\n  2,\n  1,\n  7,\n  6,\n  9\n]\n</code></pre>\n<p>Similarly for your second query:</p>\n<pre><code>.DailyForecasts | map(.AirAndPollen[1].Value)\n</code></pre>\n<p>or</p>\n<pre><code>[.DailyForecasts.[].AirAndPollen[1].Value]\n</code></pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-json prettyprint-override\"><code>[\n  12,\n  12,\n  13,\n  29,\n  69\n]\n</code></pre>\n<p>Note that your code is very sensitive to the order of the elements in the array. It looks like you are interested in the &quot;Grass&quot; value (the second element in the array, <code>.[1]</code>), so I would suggest to filter for this element specifically:</p>\n<pre><code>.DailyForecasts\n| map(.AirAndPollen.[] | select(.Name==&quot;Grass&quot;).Value)\n</code></pre>\n"
    },
    {
      "owner": {
        "account_id": 5006155,
        "reputation": 4704,
        "user_id": 4023764,
        "user_type": "registered",
        "profile_image": "https://i.sstatic.net/nE0GW.jpg?s=256",
        "display_name": "eskwayrd",
        "link": "https://stackoverflow.com/users/4023764/eskwayrd"
      },
      "is_accepted": false,
      "score": 0,
      "last_activity_date": 1712161565,
      "creation_date": 1712161565,
      "answer_id": 78269109,
      "question_id": 78267487,
      "content_license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "body": "<p>Pandoc knows how to convert content, but not presentation so much. The HTML implementing the formula is mostly presentation markup.</p>\n<p>That particular formula is very straightforward, so it is easy to convert manually:</p>\n<pre><code>[latexmath]\n++++\nL{m\\_sat} = L{m}\n++++\n</code></pre>\n<p>You likely have other formulas that require more effort to express correctly. Depending on the number of formulas, manual conversion could be notably faster than finding or implementing a tool to perform the conversion.</p>\n<p>Markdown is more prevalent than Asciidoc. So I tried a few online HTML-&gt;Markdown converters. This one seems promising: <a href=\"https://codebeautify.org/html-to-markdown\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://codebeautify.org/html-to-markdown</a></p>\n<p>It converted your HTML to <code>L m \\_ sat \\= L m</code>. Re-introducing STEM markup to recreate the rendered formula is still required, but the output seems almost good enough for a first pass.</p>\n<p>That approach might be useful if you have hundreds to thousands of formulas to convert.</p>\n"
    },
    {
      "owner": {
        "account_id": 39208,
        "reputation": 270833,
        "user_id": 112968,
        "user_type": "registered",
        "accept_rate": 68,
        "profile_image": "https://i.sstatic.net/zHTaT.png?s=256",
        "display_name": "knittl",
        "link": "https://stackoverflow.com/users/112968/knittl"
      },
      "is_accepted": false,
      "score": 0,
      "last_activity_date": 1712676006,
      "creation_date": 1712676006,
      "answer_id": 78299501,
      "question_id": 78296361,
      "content_license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "body": "<p>I think the following jq program will generate your desired output format. I had to make a few assumptions, because <code>source</code> is nowhere to be found in your input and your output doc is not valid JSON.</p>\n<pre><code>to_entries | map({\n    page_content: .value.page_data,\n    metadata: (.value | {\n        source: &quot;/content/json_data.json&quot;,\n        page_name,\n        page_url\n    } + {seq_num:(.key+1)})\n})\n</code></pre>\n<p>or</p>\n<pre><code>to_entries | map((.value | {\n    page_content: .page_data,\n    metadata: {\n        source: &quot;/content/json_data.json&quot;,\n        page_name,\n        page_url\n    }\n}) * { metadata: { seq_num: (.key+1) } })\n</code></pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-json prettyprint-override\"><code>[\n  {\n    &quot;page_content&quot;: &quot;Content about homepage&quot;,\n    &quot;metadata&quot;: {\n      &quot;source&quot;: &quot;/content/json_data.json&quot;,\n      &quot;page_name&quot;: &quot;Homepage&quot;,\n      &quot;page_url&quot;: &quot;https://mywebsite.com/homepage&quot;,\n      &quot;seq_num&quot;: 1\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    &quot;page_content&quot;: &quot;Content about Contact US&quot;,\n    &quot;metadata&quot;: {\n      &quot;source&quot;: &quot;/content/json_data.json&quot;,\n      &quot;page_name&quot;: &quot;Contact US&quot;,\n      &quot;page_url&quot;: &quot;https://mywebsite.com/contactus&quot;,\n      &quot;seq_num&quot;: 1\n    }\n  }\n]\n</code></pre>\n"
    },
    {
      "owner": {
        "account_id": 2821493,
        "reputation": 23453,
        "user_id": 2425163,
        "user_type": "registered",
        "accept_rate": 75,
        "profile_image": "https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/05f4000597865eeaf51c32ed5694479c?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG",
        "display_name": "tarleb",
        "link": "https://stackoverflow.com/users/2425163/tarleb"
      },
      "is_accepted": true,
      "score": 2,
      "last_activity_date": 1712729006,
      "creation_date": 1712729006,
      "answer_id": 78302381,
      "question_id": 78300525,
      "content_license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "body": "<p>Pandoc uses LaTeX and the <em>fancyvrb</em> package to render highlighted code blocks. We can customize the output by configuring the <code>Highlighting</code> environment used by pandoc. That's possible by placing a raw LaTeX snippet at the start of the document (or anywhere, as long as it comes before the first code block).</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-latex prettyprint-override\"><code>\\DefineVerbatimEnvironment{Highlighting}{Verbatim}{%\n  xleftmargin=18pt,commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>You'll probably have to experiment a bit and should adjust the value of <code>xleftmargin</code> to your liking. See the package documentation of <a href=\"https://ctan.org/pkg/fancyvrb\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">fancyvrb</a> for more customization options.</p>\n"
    },
    {
      "owner": {
        "account_id": 9629731,
        "reputation": 52292,
        "user_id": 10248678,
        "user_type": "registered",
        "profile_image": "https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/d8f818402f22d485b135a3b685ef908e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2",
        "display_name": "oguz ismail",
        "link": "https://stackoverflow.com/users/10248678/oguz-ismail"
      },
      "is_accepted": false,
      "score": 1,
      "last_activity_date": 1712768225,
      "creation_date": 1712768225,
      "answer_id": 78305953,
      "question_id": 78305924,
      "content_license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "body": "<p>Almost there</p>\n<pre><code>jq -c '.Documents[]' response.json\n</code></pre>\n<p><sup><a href=\"https://jqplay.org/s/z43MDGiKi4F\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Online demo</a></sup></p>\n"
    },
    {
      "owner": {
        "account_id": 18100861,
        "reputation": 41,
        "user_id": 13158728,
        "user_type": "registered",
        "profile_image": "https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f34a1a4a4b22b26b761e25068d7893a6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2",
        "display_name": "Vivek Verma",
        "link": "https://stackoverflow.com/users/13158728/vivek-verma"
